Kubernetes Tip: Stop Old ReplicaSets from Piling Up!

Ever noticed your cluster cluttered with old ReplicaSets showing 0/0/0 pods? Here's what's happening and how to fix it. 🧵

🤔 The Problem:
After multiple deployments, you'll see this:
NAME                  DESIRED   CURRENT   READY   AGE
backend-5d4c8d9f7b    3         3         3       2d   ← Active
backend-7f9c6b8d4e    0         0         0       5d   ← Old
backend-8a3d7c9e5f    0         0         0       8d   ← Old
backend-9b4e8d0f6a    0         0         0       12d  ← Old
backend-3c2a1b7e4d    0         0         0       18d  ← Old

Why does Kubernetes keep these?

Quick rollback capability (kubectl rollout undo)
Deployment audit trail

Default: Keeps 10 old ReplicaSets per Deployment!

✅ The Solution: revisionHistoryLimit

Add this to your Deployment spec:

apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: backend
spec:
  revisionHistoryLimit: 3  # Keep only 3 old versions
  replicas: 2
  template:
    # ... your pod spec
Configuration Options:
revisionHistoryLimit: 0 → No history (immediate cleanup)
revisionHistoryLimit: 3 → Keep 3 versions (recommended)
revisionHistoryLimit: 10 → Default Kubernetes behavior

🎯 Best Practices by Environment:

📊 Production: 5 - Balance between rollback capability and cleanup 📊 Staging: 3 - Moderate history 📊 Development: 1-2 - Minimal clutter

🔧 For Helm Charts:

# values.yaml
deployment:
  revisionHistoryLimit: 3

# templates/deployment.yaml
spec:
  revisionHistoryLimit: {{ .Values.deployment.revisionHistoryLimit }}

⚡ Quick Fixes:

Update existing deployment:
kubectl patch deployment backend -p '{"spec":{"revisionHistoryLimit":3}}'
Update all deployments:
kubectl get deployments -o name | xargs -I {} \
  kubectl patch {} -p '{"spec":{"revisionHistoryLimit":3}}'

💡 Key Takeaway:
Small configuration, big impact! This simple setting keeps your cluster clean, reduces etcd storage, and maintains enough history for safe rollbacks.
